From: yinjiayi <yinjiayi@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Emit complete octal escapes and advance over following digits

Use exactly three octal digits, so ASCII @ (0100) cannot become \10 and
following digits cannot be consumed as part of the escape. Advance over
all safe characters after an escape: otherwise a following decimal digit
is neither escaped nor consumed. Character classification requires an
unsigned-char value or EOF.
Original build retained; downstream target-only argv roundtrip tests cover
every non-NUL ASCII byte, including @ followed by digits.

License: ISC
Upstream-Status: Not submitted (downstream package correctness)
Removal: Drop when a fixed official release passes the same byte regressions.
--- a/sshexec.c
+++ b/sshexec.c
@@ -182,2 +182 @@
-#define IS_ALWAYS_SAFE(C)   (isalnum((C)) || (C) == '_' || (C) == '/')
-#define IS_INITIAL_SAFE(C)  (isalpha((C)) || (C) == '_' || (C) == '/')
+#define IS_ALWAYS_SAFE(C)   (isalnum((unsigned char)(C)) || (C) == '_' || (C) == '/')
@@ -228 +227 @@
-			command_len += (command[command_len] != '0');
+			command_len++;
@@ -230 +229 @@
-			command_len += (command[command_len] != '0');
+			command_len++;
@@ -237 +236 @@
-		while (IS_INITIAL_SAFE(arg[n]) || arg[n] == '_' || arg[n] == '/')
+		while (IS_ALWAYS_SAFE(arg[n]))
@@ -258 +256,0 @@
-#undef IS_INITIAL_SAFE
